\begin{abstract}
We study a two-architecture read-out model attached to a branching node of
arity $n$. An \emph{address channel}
$\Aa(n)=\log_2 n-1-\delta(n)$ pays an additive penalty equal to an entropy
deficit $\delta(n)=1/n^{2}$, while an \emph{exchange channel}
$\Xx(n)=2\bigl(1-\delta(n)\bigr)$ suffers the same deficit multiplicatively.
For integral arity, $\delta(n)$ is exactly the collision probability of two
independent uniform probes of the ordered branch-pair set $[n]\times[n]$, so
the two architectures are degraded by one and the same physical quantity in
two different grammars.

Our central structural observation is that the comparison collapses onto a
single scalar functional, the \emph{resonance} $\Rr(n)=\log_2 n+1/n^{2}$, via
the exact identity $\Aa(n)-\Xx(n)=\Rr(n)-3$. We prove that $\Rr$ is strictly
increasing on $[2,\infty)$ and strictly decreasing on $(0,1]$, and that at
integral arity $m\ge 2$ the comparison is \emph{exactly} one integer
inequality per side: $\Xx(m)<\Aa(m)$ if and only if $2^{3m^{2}-1}<m^{m^{2}}$.
The two decisive certificates are $7^{49}<2^{146}$ and $2^{191}<8^{64}$.

From these we obtain: $\Aa(n)<\Xx(n)$ for all real $2<n\le 7$;
$\Aa(n)>\Xx(n)$ for all real $n\ge 8$; a \emph{unique} crossing $r$ with
$7<r<8$ on the physical range $n>2$, settling the uniqueness conjecture;
strict monotonicity of the ratio $\Aa/\Xx$ on $[2,\infty)$, which closes the
monotonicity gap; and divergence $\Aa/\Xx\to\infty$. Extending to the full
positive axis we determine the crossing spectrum completely:
$\{n>0:\Aa(n)=\Xx(n)\}=\{1/2,r\}$, where the sub-critical crossing at
$n=1/2$ is \emph{exact} --- there $\log_2 n+\delta(n)=-1+4=3$ and both
channels equal $-6$. Finally, two integer power certificates,
$253^{64009}<2^{511048}$ and $2^{2309345}<507^{257049}$, sharpen the
transcendental crossing to the dyadic bracket $253/32<r<507/64$, that is
$7.90625<r<7.921875$.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} fork channel, entropy deficit, collision
probability, resonance functional, crossing uniqueness, integer certificates,
dyadic bracketing, unimodality.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem}

A branching node with $n$ outgoing branches --- a \emph{fork of arity $n$} ---
must be read out. Two elementary architectures suggest themselves, and they
respond to the node's imperfection in structurally different ways.

The \textbf{address channel} names branches. Naming one of $n$ branches costs
$\log_2 n$ bits, of which one bit is consumed by the fork decision itself. The
imperfection of the node, quantified by an \emph{entropy deficit} $\delta(n)$,
is charged as a straight subtraction from the payload:
\[
  \Aa(n)=\log_2 n-1-\delta(n).
\]
The \textbf{exchange channel} does not name. It always transmits a fixed
two-bit packet, but the node's imperfection degrades its \emph{rate},
multiplicatively:
\[
  \Xx(n)=2\bigl(1-\delta(n)\bigr).
\]
The same physical quantity $\delta(n)$ enters both expressions; only the
grammar of degradation differs. The question is which channel is better and,
much harder, how many times the answer changes as $n$ ranges over the arities.

Numerically the exchange channel dominates for small forks, and the address
channel eventually dominates because $\log_2 n\to\infty$ while $\Xx(n)<2$ for
all $n>0$. A sign change is therefore forced. Sampling shows it occurs between
$n=7$ and $n=8$; what sampling cannot establish is that this is the
\emph{only} sign change.

\subsection{Results}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Collapse.} $\Aa(n)-\Xx(n)=\Rr(n)-3$ where
      $\Rr(n)=\log_2 n+\delta(n)$.
\item \textbf{Monotonicity.} $\Rr$ is strictly increasing on $[2,\infty)$ and
      strictly decreasing on $(0,1]$; on $[1,2]$ it satisfies $\Rr\le 2$.
\item \textbf{Integer criterion.} For integral $m\ge 2$:
      $\Xx(m)<\Aa(m)\iff 2^{3m^{2}-1}<m^{m^{2}}$, with decisive certificates
      $7^{49}<2^{146}$ and $2^{191}<8^{64}$.
\item \textbf{Sign dichotomy.} $\Aa<\Xx$ on $(2,7]$; $\Xx<\Aa$ on
      $[8,\infty)$.
\item \textbf{Uniqueness.} Exactly one $r>2$ with $\Aa(r)=\Xx(r)$, and
      $7<r<8$.
\item \textbf{Ratio.} $\Aa/\Xx$ is strictly increasing on $[2,\infty)$ and
      tends to $\infty$.
\item \textbf{Complete spectrum.} $\{n>0:\Aa(n)=\Xx(n)\}=\{1/2,r\}$ with
      $\Aa(1/2)=\Xx(1/2)=-6$ exactly.
\item \textbf{Sharpened bracket.} $253/32<r<507/64$, certified by two integer
      power inequalities.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Method}

Three ideas carry the paper. First, the \emph{collapse}: an algebraic identity
reducing a two-function comparison to a one-function level set. Second,
\emph{unimodality}: the resonance functional has exactly one interior minimum,
capping the number of crossings at two before any numerics. Third,
\emph{integer certification}: every decisive comparison reduces to an
inequality between two integer powers, so no approximation error can enter.

\section{The model}

Throughout, $\log_2$ denotes the base-$2$ logarithm and $\ln$ the natural
logarithm.

\begin{definition}[Entropy deficit]
For $n\neq 0$, $\;\delta(n)=1/n^{2}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Address channel]
$\Aa(n)=\log_2 n-1-\delta(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Exchange channel]
$\Xx(n)=2\bigl(1-\delta(n)\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Resonance]
$\Rr(n)=\log_2 n+\delta(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Channel ratio]
$\rho(n)=\Aa(n)/\Xx(n)$, wherever $\Xx(n)\neq 0$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Combinatorial grounding of the deficit}

\begin{proposition}[Deficit as fork collision probability]
Let $n\ge 1$ be an integer and $S=[n]\times[n]$ the set of ordered pairs of
branches, $|S|=n^{2}$. Draw two elements of $S$ independently and uniformly.
The probability that they coincide is $\delta(n)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The favourable outcomes form the diagonal of $S\times S$, of size $|S|=n^{2}$;
the total is $|S|^{2}=(n^{2})^{2}$. Hence the collision probability is
$n^{2}/(n^{2})^{2}=1/n^{2}=\delta(n)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Elementary properties of the exchange channel}

\begin{lemma}[Sharpened finite-$n$ identity]
For $n\neq0$, $\;\Xx(n)\,n^{2}=2n^{2}-2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\Xx(n)n^{2}=2(1-n^{-2})n^{2}=2n^{2}-2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity and ceiling]\label{lem:ceiling}
For $n>1$, $\;0<\Xx(n)$; for $n>0$, $\;\Xx(n)<2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $n>1$ then $n^{2}>1$, so $\delta(n)<1$ and $\Xx(n)=2(1-\delta(n))>0$. If
$n>0$ then $\delta(n)>0$, so $\Xx(n)<2$.
\end{proof}

The ceiling $\Xx<2$ is the structural reason the address channel must
eventually win.

\section{The collapse}

\begin{theorem}[Collapse identity]\label{thm:collapse}
For every $n\neq0$, $\;\Aa(n)-\Xx(n)=\Rr(n)-3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $\delta=\delta(n)$,
\[
  \Aa(n)-\Xx(n)=(\log_2 n-1-\delta)-(2-2\delta)=\log_2 n+\delta-3=\Rr(n)-3.
  \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

The additive deficit contributes $-\delta$ and the multiplicative deficit
$+2\delta$; they reinforce rather than cancel, leaving exactly $+\delta$ in
the difference. That is why the difference is a genuine competition between
two terms of opposite monotonicity.

\begin{corollary}[Comparison trichotomy]\label{cor:trichotomy}
For every $n\neq0$,
\[
  \Aa(n)<\Xx(n)\iff \Rr(n)<3,\qquad
  \Xx(n)<\Aa(n)\iff \Rr(n)>3,\qquad
  \Aa(n)=\Xx(n)\iff \Rr(n)=3.
\]
\end{corollary}

\section{Derivatives and the shape of the resonance}

\begin{lemma}[Derivatives]\label{lem:derivs}
For $n\neq0$,
\[
  \delta'(n)=-\frac{2}{n^{3}},\qquad
  \Rr'(n)=\frac{1}{n\ln 2}-\frac{2}{n^{3}},\qquad
  \Aa'(n)=\frac{1}{n\ln 2}+\frac{2}{n^{3}},\qquad
  \Xx'(n)=\frac{4}{n^{3}}.
\]
\end{lemma}

Both $\Aa'>0$ and $\Xx'>0$ on $n>0$: the competition is between two increasing
functions, so no naive sign argument settles it.

\begin{lemma}[Sign of $\Rr'$]\label{lem:signR}
For $n>0$, $\;\Rr'(n)>0$ if and only if $n^{2}>2\ln 2$, i.e.
$n>\sqrt{2\ln 2}=1.1774\ldots$
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\Rr'(n)>0\iff 1/(n\ln2)>2/n^{3}\iff n^{3}>2n\ln2\iff n^{2}>2\ln2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity on the physical range]\label{thm:mono}
$\Rr$ is strictly increasing on $[2,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $n\ge2$ we have $n^{3}\ge 4n$, while $2n\ln 2<1.3863\,n<4n$; hence
$n^{3}>2n\ln 2$, i.e. $n^{2}>2\ln2$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:signR} gives
$\Rr'>0$ on $(2,\infty)$. A function continuous on an interval with positive
derivative on its interior is strictly increasing there.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict antitonicity on sub-critical arity]\label{thm:anti}
$\Rr$ is strictly decreasing on $(0,1]$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $0<n\le1$, $n^{2}\le 1<1.3862\ldots=2\ln2$, so $\Rr'<0$ on $(0,1)$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:signR}; continuity on $(0,1]$ finishes the argument.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[The separating interval is inert]\label{lem:inert}
For $1\le n\le2$, $\;\Rr(n)\le2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\log_2 n\le\log_2 2=1$ and $\delta(n)=1/n^{2}\le1$.
\end{proof}

Together these say that $\Rr$ is \emph{unimodal} on $(0,\infty)$ with a single
interior minimum at $n_\star=\sqrt{2\ln2}\approx1.1774$, where
$\Rr(n_\star)\approx0.9570$, and that the transition band $[1,2]$ lies
strictly below the critical level $3$. A unimodal function meets a horizontal
line strictly above its minimum in at most two points.

\section{The integer criterion}

\begin{lemma}[Clearing the logarithm]\label{lem:clear}
For $n>0$,
\[
  \Rr(n)<3\iff (\ln n)\,n^{2}<\bigl(3n^{2}-1\bigr)\ln2,\qquad
  \Rr(n)>3\iff \bigl(3n^{2}-1\bigr)\ln2<(\ln n)\,n^{2}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $\Rr(n)=\dfrac{n^{2}\ln n+\ln2}{n^{2}\ln2}$. As $n^{2}\ln2>0$, the
inequality $\Rr(n)<3$ is equivalent to $n^{2}\ln n+\ln2<3n^{2}\ln2$, i.e. to
$n^{2}\ln n<(3n^{2}-1)\ln2$; similarly for the reverse.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exponentiation]\label{lem:exp}
For an integer $m\ge2$,
$\;(\ln m)\,m^{2}<(3m^{2}-1)\ln2\iff m^{m^{2}}<2^{3m^{2}-1}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\ln(m^{m^{2}})=m^{2}\ln m$ and $\ln(2^{3m^{2}-1})=(3m^{2}-1)\ln2$, and $\ln$
is strictly increasing on positive reals.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integer criterion: one inequality per side]\label{thm:intcrit}
Let $m\ge2$ be an integer. Then
\[
  \Aa(m)<\Xx(m)\iff m^{m^{2}}<2^{3m^{2}-1},
  \qquad
  \Xx(m)<\Aa(m)\iff 2^{3m^{2}-1}<m^{m^{2}}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Corollary~\ref{cor:trichotomy} with Lemmas~\ref{lem:clear}
and~\ref{lem:exp}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Decisive certificates]\label{prop:certs}
$7^{49}<2^{146}$ and $2^{191}<8^{64}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For the second, $8^{64}=(2^{3})^{64}=2^{192}>2^{191}$. For the first,
$7^{49}\approx2.569\times10^{41}$ has $42$ decimal digits while
$2^{146}\approx8.920\times10^{43}$ has $44$; the comparison is a finite exact
integer computation.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:78}
$\Aa(7)<\Xx(7)$ and $\Xx(8)<\Aa(8)$; equivalently $\Rr(7)<3<\Rr(8)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:intcrit} at $m=7$ ($m^{2}=49$, $3m^{2}-1=146$) and
$m=8$ ($m^{2}=64$, $3m^{2}-1=191$), then
Corollary~\ref{cor:trichotomy}.
\end{proof}

Numerically $\Rr(7)=2.82776\ldots$ and $\Rr(8)=3+\tfrac1{64}$ exactly. The
margin at $n=8$ is one part in sixty-four, which is precisely why an exact
certificate is preferable to a numerical estimate.

\section{Sign dichotomy and uniqueness}

\begin{theorem}[Negative side]\label{thm:neg}
For every real $n$ with $2<n\le7$, $\;\Aa(n)<\Xx(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Rr$ is strictly increasing on $[2,\infty)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}), so
$\Rr(n)\le\Rr(7)<3$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:78}; apply
Corollary~\ref{cor:trichotomy}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Positive side]\label{thm:pos}
For every real $n\ge8$, $\;\Xx(n)<\Aa(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\Rr(n)\ge\Rr(8)>3$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:mono} and Corollary~\ref{cor:78}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniqueness of the crossing]\label{thm:unique}
There is exactly one real $r>2$ with $\Aa(r)=\Xx(r)$, and $7<r<8$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Existence.} $\Rr$ is continuous on $[7,8]$ and $\Rr(7)<3<\Rr(8)$, so by
the intermediate value theorem there is $r\in[7,8]$ with $\Rr(r)=3$, whence
$\Aa(r)=\Xx(r)$.

\emph{Uniqueness.} If $y>2$ also satisfies $\Aa(y)=\Xx(y)$ then $\Rr(y)=3=\Rr(r)$
with $y,r\in[2,\infty)$, and a strictly increasing function is injective, so
$y=r$.

\emph{Location.} If $r\le7$, Theorem~\ref{thm:neg} contradicts
$\Aa(r)=\Xx(r)$; if $r\ge8$, Theorem~\ref{thm:pos} does. Hence $7<r<8$.
\end{proof}

Numerically $r=7.9119050\ldots$

\section{The ratio \texorpdfstring{$\Aa/\Xx$}{A/X}}

\begin{theorem}[Strict monotonicity of the ratio]\label{thm:ratio}
The ratio $\rho=\Aa/\Xx$ is strictly increasing on $[2,\infty)$, in particular
on $[8,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
On $n\ge2$ we have $\Xx(n)>0$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:ceiling}), so
$\rho'=(\Aa'\Xx-\Aa\Xx')/\Xx^{2}$. Substituting Lemma~\ref{lem:derivs} and
simplifying with $\Xx(n)n^{2}=2n^{2}-2$,
\[
  \Aa'(n)\Xx(n)-\Aa(n)\Xx'(n)
  =\frac{2\bigl[(n^{2}-1)+4\ln2-2\ln n\bigr]}{n^{3}\ln2}.
\]
The bracket is positive for all $n>0$: from $\ln n\le n-1$ we get
$2\ln n\le 2n-2\le n^{2}-1$, the last step being $(n-1)^{2}\ge0$; and
$4\ln2>0$ strictly. Hence $\rho'>0$ on $(2,\infty)$ and $\rho$ is continuous
on $[2,\infty)$.
\end{proof}

The bracket attains its minimum value $4\ln2\approx2.77$ at $n=1$, so the
positivity is comfortable rather than marginal.

\begin{lemma}[Lower bound on the ratio]\label{lem:ratiolb}
For $n\ge8$, $\;\rho(n)\ge(\log_2 n-2)/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For $n\ge8$, $\delta(n)\le1$ gives $\Aa(n)\ge\log_2 n-2\ge1>0$, and
$0<\Xx(n)<2$ gives $\rho(n)\ge \Aa(n)/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Divergence]\label{thm:div}
$\rho(n)\to\infty$ as $n\to\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(\log_2 n-2)/2\to\infty$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:ratiolb} bounds $\rho$ from below
eventually.
\end{proof}

\section{The complete crossing spectrum}

\begin{theorem}[The exact sub-critical crossing]\label{thm:half}
At $n=1/2$ we have $\log_2\tfrac12+\delta(\tfrac12)=-1+4=3$ and
$\Aa(\tfrac12)=\Xx(\tfrac12)=-6$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\log_2(1/2)=-1$ and $\delta(1/2)=4$, so $\Rr(1/2)=3$; then
$\Aa(1/2)=-1-1-4=-6$ and $\Xx(1/2)=2(1-4)=-6$.
\end{proof}

This is the \emph{hidden resonance} between the logarithm and the entropy
deficit: an exact integer coincidence between a dyadic logarithm and a dyadic
deficit, available in closed form only because $1/2$ is a power of $2$.

\begin{theorem}[Complete crossing spectrum]\label{thm:spectrum}
There is a real $r$ with $7<r<8$ such that
\[
  \{\,n>0:\Aa(n)=\Xx(n)\,\}=\{\,\tfrac12,\;r\,\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:trichotomy} the set equals $\{n>0:\Rr(n)=3\}$. Let $n>0$
with $\Rr(n)=3$. If $n\le1$, then $\Rr$ is injective on $(0,1]$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:anti}) and $\Rr(1/2)=3$, so $n=1/2$. If $1<n<2$, then
Lemma~\ref{lem:inert} gives $\Rr(n)\le2<3$, a contradiction. If $n\ge2$, then
$\Rr$ is injective on $[2,\infty)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}) and $\Rr(r)=3$
with $r\ge2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}), so $n=r$. Conversely both points lie
in the set.
\end{proof}

Two crossings, not one and not three: the count is forced by the shape of
$\Rr$, not by a numerical accident. In particular, the expectation of a second
crossing at small arity is false on the physical range $n>2$, but true and
exactly solvable on the analytic continuation to $n>0$.

\section{Bracketing the transcendental crossing}

\begin{lemma}[Logarithm bounds from integer powers]\label{lem:logbounds}
Let $x>0$ and let $p,q$ be positive integers. If $x^{q}<2^{p}$ then
$\log_2 x<p/q$; if $2^{p}<x^{q}$ then $p/q<\log_2 x$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Take $\ln$ of both sides and divide by $q\ln 2>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Bracketing certificates]\label{prop:brackcerts}
$253^{64009}<2^{511048}$ and $2^{2309345}<507^{257049}$.
\end{proposition}

These are exact integer comparisons; the numbers have roughly $1.5\times10^{5}$
and $7\times10^{5}$ decimal digits. The exponents are $q=253^{2}=64009$ and
$q=507^{2}=257049$.

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic bracket]\label{thm:bracket}
$\dfrac{253}{32}<r<\dfrac{507}{64}$, i.e. $7.90625<r<7.921875$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $n_-=253/32$: since $32=2^{5}$, $\log_2 n_-=\log_2 253-5$ and
$\delta(n_-)=1024/64009$. Lemma~\ref{lem:logbounds} with the first certificate
gives $\log_2 253<511048/64009$, so
\[
  \Rr(n_-)<\frac{511048}{64009}-5+\frac{1024}{64009}
  =\frac{512072}{64009}-5=8-5=3 .
\]
At $n_+=507/64$: since $64=2^{6}$, $\log_2 n_+=\log_2 507-6$ and
$\delta(n_+)=4096/257049$. The second certificate gives
$\log_2 507>2309345/257049$, so
\[
  \Rr(n_+)>\frac{2309345}{257049}-6+\frac{4096}{257049}
  =\frac{2313441}{257049}-6=9-6=3 .
\]
Since $\Rr$ is strictly increasing on $[2,\infty)$ with $\Rr(r)=3$, the claim
follows.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[The general dyadic recipe]
For integers $a\ge1$ and $k\ge0$, $\Rr(a/2^{k})=\log_2 a-k+4^{k}/a^{2}$, so
\[
  \Rr\!\left(\frac{a}{2^{k}}\right)<3
  \iff a^{\,a^{2}}<2^{\,(3+k)a^{2}-4^{k}},
\]
an inequality between two integers with no logarithms at all. With $a=253$,
$k=5$: $a^{2}=64009$ and $8\cdot64009-1024=511048$. With $a=507$, $k=6$:
$a^{2}=257049$ and $9\cdot257049-4096=2309345$. Every dyadic bracket, at any
precision, is certified this way by one integer power comparison per endpoint.
The true value is $r=7.91190503766\ldots$
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Exact integer arity test.}
Given an integer $m\ge2$, compute $m^{m^{2}}$ and $2^{3m^{2}-1}$ as exact
integers and compare; by Theorem~\ref{thm:intcrit} this is a complete decision
procedure with no numerical error. Both numbers have $\Theta(m^{2}\log m)$
bits, so the cost is $O(m^{2}\log m)$ multiplications by repeated squaring on
numbers of that size.

\paragraph{Certified dyadic bracketing.}
Bisect on the dyadic grid $\{a/2^{k}\}$, deciding the sign of
$\Rr(a/2^{k})-3$ by the exact integer test of the remark above. The bisection
needs $O(k)$ tests and yields a rigorously certified bracket; at $k=5,6$ it
gives $253/32<r<507/64$.

\paragraph{High-precision refinement.}
Newton's method on $F(n)=\Rr(n)-3$, with $F'(n)=1/(n\ln2)-2/n^{3}>0$ on
$[7,8]$, converges quadratically; three iterations from $n_0=7.9$ give $r$ to
more than twelve digits. Certified bracketing then confirms the digits.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What made uniqueness hard}
Existence of a crossing is a two-point computation; uniqueness is global, and
no finite sampling implies it. The global control here is structural: the
difference $\Aa-\Xx$ is, up to an additive constant, a single functional whose
derivative changes sign exactly once on $(0,\infty)$. Once unimodality is in
hand, the crossing count is at most two before any numbers are examined.

The pattern is general: whenever two architectures are degraded by the same
quantity, one additively and one multiplicatively, the difference of their
performances is a linear combination of the degradation and whatever else
distinguishes them, so the comparison becomes a level-set problem for one
functional. Counting solutions is then a question about the \emph{shape} of
that functional.

\subsection{Why integer certificates}
The critical margin at $n=8$ is $\Rr(8)-3=1/64$; the dyadic bracketing
operates on margins of order $10^{-5}$ and below. Reducing every decisive
comparison to an inequality between two integers eliminates all questions of
numerical trust: $2^{191}<8^{64}$ is either true or false, and it is
humanly checkable because $8^{64}=2^{192}$.

\subsection{Robustness of the model}
Nothing in the collapse used the exponent $2$ in $\delta(n)=1/n^{2}$. For any
$s>0$, with $\delta_s(n)=n^{-s}$ one gets
$\Aa_s(n)-\Xx_s(n)=\Rr_s(n)-3$ where $\Rr_s(n)=\log_2 n+n^{-s}$, and
$\Rr_s'(n)=\frac{1}{n\ln2}-s\,n^{-s-1}$ vanishes exactly once, at
$n^{s}=s\ln2$. The whole family is unimodal, and its minimum value simplifies
remarkably: writing $u=s\ln2$, the minimiser is $n_\star=u^{1/s}$ and
\[
  \Rr_s^{\min}=\frac{\log_2 u}{s}+\frac1u=\frac{\ln u}{u}+\frac1u
  =\frac{1+\ln u}{u}.
\]
Since $\frac{d}{du}\frac{1+\ln u}{u}=-\frac{\ln u}{u^{2}}$, this is maximised at
$u=1$ with value exactly $1$.

\begin{proposition}[Universal valley bound]
For every $s>0$, $\;\Rr_s^{\min}\le1$, with equality precisely when
$s=1/\ln2=\log_2 e=1.4427\ldots$
\end{proposition}

Since the critical level $3$ exceeds $1$, it clears the valley floor for
\emph{every} deficit exponent: the two-crossing structure is not an artefact of
$s=2$ but a universal feature of the family; indeed any level $c>1$ is met
exactly twice, for every $s>0$. For $s=2$ one has $u=2\ln2\approx1.386$ and
$\Rr_2^{\min}\approx0.957$.

\subsection{The dyadic coincidence}
The exact crossing at $n=1/2$ arises because $\log_2$ and $\delta$ are
simultaneously rational at powers of two: $\Rr(2^{-k})=4^{k}-k$. The critical
level $3$ is hit at $k=1$ because $4-1=3$. Since $k\mapsto4^{k}-k$ is strictly
increasing for $k\ge1$, taking the values $1,3,14,61,252,\dots$ at
$k=0,1,2,3,4$, no other dyadic arity of the form $2^{-k}$ resonates at level
$3$.

\section{Future work}

\paragraph{Dyadic rigidity of resonance levels.}
The relation $\Rr(2^{-k})=4^{k}-k$ turns the question of which integer levels
admit dyadic solutions into a Diophantine one. We conjecture that for every
integer level $c$ the equation $\Rr(n)=c$ has at most one solution of the form
$n=2^{-k}$. More generally, solutions at arbitrary dyadic rationals $a/2^{k}$
are equivalent to $a^{a^{2}}=2^{(c+k)a^{2}-4^{k}}$, solvable only when $a$ is
itself a power of two, suggesting a rigidity statement.

\paragraph{Deficit exponent families.}
Since the valley floor of $\Rr_s$ never exceeds $1$, the level $3$ is met
exactly twice for every $s>0$. Two questions follow: for which $s$ is the
sub-critical crossing exactly solvable, as it is at $s=2$ where it equals
$1/2$; and what is the asymptotic behaviour of the super-critical crossing
$r(s)$, which increases to $8$ as $s\to\infty$?

\paragraph{Higher-arity read-out models.}
Replacing the two-bit exchange packet by a $b$-bit packet changes the critical
level from $3$ to $b+1$; the crossing arity then grows roughly like $2^{b+1}$,
and the integer criterion becomes $m^{m^{2}}\lessgtr2^{(b+1)m^{2}-1}$.

\paragraph{Non-uniform forks.}
Replacing the uniform branch distribution by an arbitrary one turns $\delta$
into the R\'enyi-$2$ collision probability $\sum_i p_i^{2}$ and $\log_2 n$ into
the Shannon entropy. The collapse identity survives, and the comparison becomes
a question about the interaction of Shannon and R\'enyi-$2$ entropies on the
simplex.

\section*{Summary of results}

